<h1>
Disrupting the Undisruptable: Agentic AI Innovations in Construction
</h1>

<p class="lead">
<h2>Most cities lack affordable housing and resilient infrastructures, 
    but why does the construction industry still struggle with its productivity?</h2>

According to the United Nations’ 2018 Revision of
World Urbanisation Prospects, the world’s population living in urban areas is expected
to increase from 55% to 68% by 2050; however, the housing affordability crisis is
intensifying undesirably: 80% of cities in the world lack adequate housing (World
Green Building Council, 2023). Moreover, the sector is also often named as the cornerstone for national
economics, accounting for 13% of the global GDP with a high multiplier effect to spur
trading activities (McKinsey, 2020). Nonetheless, the global annual labour productivity
growth in construction is merely 0.4% between 2000 and 2022, contrasting
significantly from the total economic growth at 2% (McKinsey, 2025). This paradox
implies that the construction industry’s limitations in adopting technologies and
improving productivity prevent society from realising advantages of technological
innovations.

