<h2>Is agentic AI the antidote to the global housing crisis?</h2>
<p>The Anthropic Economic Index Survey launched in April/2026 collected 81,000 survey responses to study how AI reshapes economy and workforce structures.
Their findings suggest that AI can reduce task completion time by 80%; however, 1/5 of the respondents reported concerns about AI displacement.
Despite a substantial increase of AI uses, the architectural and engineering sector reduces the Claude.ai usage time from 4.7% to 2.5% in 2025 (see Figure 2).
</p>

<p>
A <em>technocentric </em> approach is beneficial for tackling low
productivity in the construction industry (Oke et al., 2023; Oti-Sarpong et al., 2022).
For instance, the UK government introduced the Digital Built Britain Strategy in 2015
to promote level 3 building information modelling (BIM), digital twins (DTs), and big
data analytics with an aim to reduce 15-20% construction costs (Centre for Digital Built
Britain, 2022). Although digitalisation progresses were observed, long-term
innovations on productivity are stagnant. BIM and DT have effectively improved
productivity rate in manufacturing and aviation, but it is still unclear why the
construction industry remained “traditional”, “slow to change”, and “unproductive” to
exploit these technologies (Liu et al., 2025). Given that the construction sector is foundational to
providing affordable housing and essential infrastructure, it attracts significant
governmental investments alongside robust private market incentives to absorb
demands.   Understanding how agentic AI disrupts and innovates this traditional
industry is therefore vital for steering societal developments.
</p>

