<h2>Disruptive Innovation Theory: Netflix, Airbnb, Apple</h2>
<p>
The Disruptive Innovation Theory (DIT) introduced by Harvard Business School Professor Clayton Christensen in 1995 is prominent
in describing the process of innovation. It refers to the innovation in which entrants
disrupt the existing market dominated by incumbents: entrants introduce simpler, more
affordable, or more accessible products or services to either underserved customers
in low-end markets or non-consumers in new markets. Entrants usually start with
inferior performances; thus, incumbents ignore their threats. However, entrants
improve their offerings, eventually attract more customers, and finally displace
incumbents by creating long-lasting disruption (Christensen et al., 2009).
Figure 3 depicts the distinguished trajectories of Disruptive Innovations and Sustaining Innovations.
Famous DIT application cases include Netflix which displaces Blockbuster, Airbnb which disrupts the hotel industry, and Apple which revolutionises smartphone designs.
</p>

<p>
A highlight from the existing studies is the that DIT framework is widely applied in the consumer products and retail industry, 
but rarely in socially impactful industry, such as medical, education, and construction industry.
We therefore ask: under what institutional conditions does agentic AI challenge existing innovation trajectories in the
construction industry? To answer this question, semi-structured interviews
were conducted with 34 construction professionals across 7 regions in the world.
5 categories of 2nd-hand archival data including (1) government, (2) technology, (3) market, (4) industry, and
(5) academic reports were gathered additionally for triangulation (Gehman et al., 2018). Data were analysed
abductively with the Gioia method to extract 1st-order concepts from interview transcripts, 
later grouped into 2nd-order themes, and aggregated into 3 final dimensions which describe the disruptive innovation process led by agentic AI in the construction industry.
</p>

