<h2>Agentic AI innovations in construction: (1) Triggers of innovation</h2>
<p>
Considering the 1st dimension, <em>Triggers of Innovation</em>, there are three specific themes contributing to the origins of innovation in the
construction industry as per our interviewees’ experiences, namely (1) institution pressure, (2)
project delivery crises, and (3) data fragmentation. 

Construction projects have long suffered from time delays and cost overruns while
failing to deliver the promised societal utility. Flyvbjerg (2017) asserted in The Oxford Handbook of Megaproject Management that
the Iron law of megaprojects is persistently crumbling the industry with the curse of
over budget, over time, under benefits, over and over again.

One of our respondents shared that <em>“The government [drafted stringent] building regulations that push us to
innovate; otherwise, age-old technologies [cannot assist us to] construct houses up to
building standards within limited time and budget constraints.”</em> 
</p>

<p>
Another interviewee expressed that <em>“every project that I am involved in has never finished within
the initial budget plan, which makes me feel anxious since I am in charge of the
financial arrangements in the company. It would be nice if there are technology that
improve cost transparency, if not fully forecast the actual cost.”</em> Agentic AI disruptiveness is exemplified in its ability to heighten decision quality by
reducing information asymmetry and data collection time from heterogeneous sources. Some of our participants agreed that
<em> “Specific agents can actually work on their [own] part and then come up with a
report that he or she can approve, and then you have a management system.”</em>
</p>

