<h2>Agentic AI innovations in construction: (3) Institution-enabled innovation </h2>
<p>
Institution refers to “the system of social factors that collectively generate a regularity
of behaviour” (Greif, 2006). Most notably, the 2024 Nobel Prize winner in economics science, 
Acemoglu (2025), classified institutions into two broad categories: (1)
economic arrangements which determine incentives, and (2) political arrangements
which distribute power. He posited that both economic and political institutions can
either be extractive or inclusive to influence technological innovation processes.
Extractive institutions, characterised by limited market competition economically and
excessive political power concentration within the social elites, stifle innovation;
inclusive institutions, on the other hand, are more likely to stimulate innovation by
providing economic incentives and protection by the rule of law, like patent registration.
</p>

<p>
Interviewees from developing countries expressed concerns about their dependence on AI infrastructure,
which is primarily developed and controlled by a small number of Western and Chinese firms, such as Google and Baidu.
Surprisingly, even some of the European respodents are apprehensive about the monopolistic nature of AI technological advancements, 
hence putting extra efforts on AI governance: <em> “We do not have our proprietary models
nor data centres [set up] by international firms like Google, Amazon Web Services.”
</em>
</p>

