<h2>Three pillars of agentic AI disruption in construction</h2>

<ul>
<p>
<li>Developing agent governance frameworks</li>
Existing technology governance policies are insufficient to tackle the surging inequalities in AI developments and deployments.
Data privacy and cybersecurity are emphasized as challenges for agentic AI use. 
Interviewees from developing countries consistently
raised concerns regarding the dominance of a “western-developed” AI landscape,
where talents, data and digital infrastructure are skewed toward the developed
countries.
Agentic AI governance policy frameworks capable of monitoring the
responsible and ethical use of data and AI agents have to be developed before its deployment.
</p>

<p>
<li>Establishing risk allocation mechanisms</li>
While market competition in state-of-the-art AI models benefit both customers and suppliers for
technological and social progresses, our findings suggest that disruptive innovation resistance is exceptionally strong in the construction industry.
Given the high-risk nature in construction projects, for instance, high capital baseline and stringent bidding requirements,
stakeholders mostly adopt a risk-averse appraoch to shield themselves from catastropic AI-deployment failures, 
which would lead to more serious societal impacts, such as fires or collapses in buildings. 

Over the years, the government have set high entry barriers into the construction sector to ensure public safety and health.
Yet, risk allocation mechanisms are still underdeveloped to address the uncertainties in agentic AI decision-making, 
especially when the AI agents are not fully explainable and auditable.
</p>

<li>Integrating industry standards for AI adoption</li>
Embedding domain knowledge is equally imperative to enhance users trust to agentic AI decision-making capabilities.
The construction industry is characterised by strong institutional mediation, in which regulatory bodies,
professional associations, and contractual frameworks determine the conditions under
which new entrants can operate independently of their technological capabilities.

The value networks for disruption are public issues by nature.
For example, in the construction industry, building standards and
cost estimation methodologies in projects are useful data sources for training the AI
agents.

</ul>

<h2>Agentic AI and society</h2>
<p>
The purpose of this study is to explore how <em> disruptive innovation</em> occurs in the context of the
“undisruptable” sectors, particularly in the construction industry. Research regarding
disruptive changes in public sectors like healthcare, education, and construction is
scarce, especially for innovations driven by AI and digital technologies.
</p>

<p>
Findings from this study provide a starting point into the critical impacts of institutions
for disruptive innovation. By drawing implications from the construction industry, our
results indicated that there is a necessity to incorporate more institution-enabled
mechanism to public-sector industries

The conceptual model developed in Figure 7 offers a deeper understanding to researchers,
professionals and policymakers on how to initiate changes in the AI era.

While our research is contextualised within the construction industry, the findings and
practical implications are potentially transferable to a broader array of public-sector
industries, for example, medical, healthcare and education.
</p>

